= Solution
{parent=1}

The two orthogonal directions $(1,1)^T$ and $(1,-1)^T$ are preserved by this <symmetric matrix>. Direct multiplication, using the <hyperbolic function> identities $\cosh u\pm\sinh u=e^{\pm u}$, gives the <eigenvalue>–<eigenvector> pairs
$$
\boxed{\lambda_+=e^u,\quad \boldsymbol v_+=\binom11;
\qquad \lambda_-=e^{-u},\quad \boldsymbol v_-=\binom1{-1}.}
$$
Each <eigenvector> can be multiplied by any nonzero scalar. For $u\ne0$ these give the two one-dimensional <eigenspaces>. When $u=0$, the <matrix> is the identity: the only <eigenvalue> is $1$, with multiplicity two, and every nonzero <vector> is an <eigenvector>.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The boundary order fixes the orientation. Two edges issuing from $A$ are $\overrightarrow{AB}=(0,0,1)$ and $\overrightarrow{AD}=(1,1,0)$. Their <cross product> gives the oriented <vector area>:
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol A=\overrightarrow{AB}\times\overrightarrow{AD}
=\begin{pmatrix}-1\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Its <norm> is $\sqrt2$, the ordinary rectangle area. The sign agrees with traversing $A\to B\to C\to D\to A$ by the right-hand orientation rule.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

For this <first-order linear differential equation>, the <integrating factor> is $e^x$. Thus
$$
\frac d{dx}(e^xy)=e^{3x},\qquad e^xy=\frac13e^{3x}+C,
$$
and the <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac13e^{2x}+Ce^{-x},\qquad C\in\mathbb R.}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

For $n=0$, the <change of variables> $s=\sqrt a\,x$ reduces the <integral> to the given <Gaussian integral>:
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-ax^2}dx
=\frac1{\sqrt a}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-s^2}ds=\sqrt{\frac\pi a}.}
$$
For $n=1$, the integrand is an integrable <odd function>, so its contributions from the two half-lines cancel:
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x e^{-ax^2}dx=0.}
$$
The condition $a>0$ ensures convergence in both cases.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Complete the square in the velocity variable:
$$
H=V(x)+pv-\frac12mv^2
=V(x)+\frac{p^2}{2m}-\frac m2\left(v-\frac pm\right)^2.
$$
Since $m>0$, the final term is nonpositive and vanishes at exactly one velocity. Therefore
$$
\boxed{v_*=\frac pm,\qquad H_{\max}(x,p)=\frac{p^2}{2m}+V(x).}
$$
This is the <Legendre transform in mechanics> that constructs the <Hamiltonian mechanics> energy from the kinetic-minus-potential Lagrangian.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Expanding the <cross product> gives
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol F\times\boldsymbol G
=\begin{pmatrix}-xz\\-yz\\x^2+y^2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Taking its <divergence> component by component,
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot(\boldsymbol F\times\boldsymbol G)
=\partial_x(-xz)+\partial_y(-yz)+\partial_z(x^2+y^2)=-2z.}
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

Normalization of the <probability mass function> and the <geometric series> give
$$
1=c\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}e^{-n}=\frac c{1-e^{-1}},
\qquad \boxed{c=1-e^{-1}.}
$$
The tail is another <geometric series>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X\geq N)
=c\sum_{n=N}^{\infty}e^{-n}
=\frac{ce^{-N}}{1-e^{-1}}=e^{-N}.}
$$

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Substitute $r=-x^2$ into the <geometric series>, without taking any <derivatives>:
$$
\boxed{\frac1{1+x^2}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}(-1)^k x^{2k}
=1-x^2+x^4-\cdots,\qquad |x|<1.}
$$
Thus the coefficient of $x^n$ in the <Taylor series> is $(-1)^{n/2}$ when $n$ is even and zero when $n$ is odd. If nonzero terms are numbered from one, the $n$th term is $(-1)^{n-1}x^{2n-2}$.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The <chain rule> gives
$$
u_t=a f'(x+at),\qquad u_x=f'(x+at),\qquad
u_t+u_x=(a+1)f'(x+at).
$$
For this to vanish for every differentiable $f$, its coefficient must be zero:
$$
\boxed{a=-1.}
$$
The resulting $f(x-t)$ is a profile transported to the right at unit speed by the <transport equation>.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
