= Solution
{parent=a}

The evaluated position <vectors> are $\boldsymbol r_1(1)=(2,1,0)$ and $\boldsymbol r_2(-1)=(-1,-1,0)$. Their difference is $(3,2,0)$, so the <Euclidean distance> is
$$
\boxed{\|\boldsymbol r_1(1)-\boldsymbol r_2(-1)\|=\sqrt{3^2+2^2}=\sqrt{13}.}
$$

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <cross product> of the direction <vectors> is $\boldsymbol n_1\times\boldsymbol n_2=(1,-1,0)$. It is nonzero, so a common perpendicular direction exists. Projecting $\boldsymbol a_2-\boldsymbol a_1=(-1,0,1)$ onto its unit <vector> gives the <distance between skew lines>:
$$
s=\frac{|(\boldsymbol a_2-\boldsymbol a_1)\cdot(\boldsymbol n_1\times\boldsymbol n_2)|}
{\|\boldsymbol n_1\times\boldsymbol n_2\|}
=\frac{|-1|}{\sqrt2}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{s=\frac1{\sqrt2}.}
$$
The numerator is a <scalar triple product>; movement along either line does not change this perpendicular projection.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use only the <dot product> to minimize the squared <Euclidean distance> from the origin:
$$
D(t)^2=(\boldsymbol a_2+t\boldsymbol n_2)\cdot(\boldsymbol a_2+t\boldsymbol n_2)
=\|\boldsymbol a_2\|^2+2t(\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2)+t^2\|\boldsymbol n_2\|^2.
$$
Completing the square gives
$$
D(t)^2=\|\boldsymbol n_2\|^2
\left(t+\frac{\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2}{\|\boldsymbol n_2\|^2}\right)^2
+\|\boldsymbol a_2\|^2-
\frac{(\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2)^2}{\|\boldsymbol n_2\|^2}.
$$
The square is minimized at $t_0=-(\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2)/\|\boldsymbol n_2\|^2$, yielding
$$
\boxed{d_0=\sqrt{\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol a_2
-\frac{(\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2)^2}{\boldsymbol n_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2}}.}
$$
Geometrically this subtracts the squared length of the <orthogonal projection> parallel to the line. At the minimizing point, $(\boldsymbol a_2+t_0\boldsymbol n_2)\cdot\boldsymbol n_2=0$, as required for perpendicular distance.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol a_2=1$, $\boldsymbol a_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2=1$ and $\boldsymbol n_2\cdot\boldsymbol n_2=3$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{d_0=\sqrt{1-\frac13}=\sqrt{\frac23}.}
$$
The closest point has parameter $t_0=-1/3$ and position $(-1/3,-1/3,2/3)$, whose <norm> gives the same result.

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a point $\boldsymbol p=\boldsymbol r_2(t)$, the magnitude of $(\boldsymbol p-\boldsymbol a_1)\times\boldsymbol n_1$ is the area of a parallelogram. Dividing by its base length $\|\boldsymbol n_1\|$ gives the perpendicular <Euclidean distance> to the first line. Explicitly,
$$
\boldsymbol p-\boldsymbol a_1=(t-1,t,t+1),\qquad
(\boldsymbol p-\boldsymbol a_1)\times\boldsymbol n_1=(-(t+1),t+1,-1).
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{d(t)=\frac{\sqrt{2(t+1)^2+1}}{\sqrt2}
=\sqrt{(t+1)^2+\frac12}.}
$$
This use of the <cross product> removes any component parallel to the first line automatically.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The graph is the upper branch of the <hyperbola> $d^2-(t+1)^2=1/2$. It is symmetric about $t=-1$, positive everywhere, and has its lowest point at $(-1,1/\sqrt2)$. Its <derivatives> are
$$
d'(t)=\frac{t+1}{d(t)},\qquad d''(t)=\frac1{2d(t)^3}>0,
$$
so it is strictly convex. Since
$$
d(t)-|t+1|=\frac{1/2}{\sqrt{(t+1)^2+1/2}+|t+1|}\longrightarrow0,
$$
its two oblique <asymptotes> are
$$
\boxed{d=t+1\quad(t\to+\infty),\qquad d=-t-1\quad(t\to-\infty).}
$$
\Image[paper-2-q11-distance.png]
{title=Distance from a moving point to a skew line}
{description=Original plot of $d(t)=\sqrt{(t+1)^2+1/2}$, its minimum and its two asymptotes.}

= iii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The square $d(t)^2=(t+1)^2+1/2$ is minimized when its first term vanishes. Therefore
$$
\boxed{t_{\min}=-1,\qquad d_{\min}=\frac1{\sqrt2}=s.}
$$
This agrees with the <distance between skew lines> found in part (b). The corresponding parameter on the first line is its <orthogonal projection> value
$$
\lambda_{\min}=\frac{(\boldsymbol r_2(-1)-\boldsymbol a_1)\cdot\boldsymbol n_1}{\|\boldsymbol n_1\|^2}=-\frac32.
$$
The closest pair is $(-1/2,-3/2,0)$ and $(-1,-1,0)$; their connecting <vector> $(-1/2,1/2,0)$ is perpendicular to both direction <vectors>.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
