= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At a differentiable interior <stationary point>, all first-order changes of the scalar field vanish. In particular, the <directional derivative> along each coordinate axis vanishes, so $f_x=f_y=0$ and hence $\nabla f=\boldsymbol0$. Equivalently, the first-order differential $df=\nabla f\cdot d\boldsymbol r$ vanishes for every displacement. A differentiable interior maximum or minimum must satisfy this condition; a stationary point need not be an extremum.

The standard nondegenerate local <contour plot> models are $-x^2-y^2$ for a maximum, $x^2+y^2$ for a minimum, and $x^2-y^2$ for a <saddle point of a scalar function>. Near a maximum, nested contours have lower values farther from the centre; near a minimum they have higher values. Near a saddle, <hyperbolas> occur on opposite sides of the critical level, whose contour has two intersecting tangent directions. More general nondegenerate quadratic forms deform these circles into <ellipses>; degenerate stationary points can have more complicated contours.

\Image[paper-2-q12-stationary-contours.png]
{title=Local contours near a maximum, a minimum and a saddle}
{description=Original quadratic models, with signed contour labels showing how the field changes away from each stationary point.}

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Set $D=1+x^2+y^2>0$. The <quotient rule> gives the <partial derivatives>
$$
f_x=-\frac{4x(1+y+y^2)}{D^2},\qquad
f_y=\frac{2(1+x^2-y^2+2x^2y)}{D^2}.
$$
Since $1+y+y^2=(y+1/2)^2+3/4>0$, the first equation $f_x=0$ forces $x=0$. The second then reduces to $1-y^2=0$. Thus the only <stationary points> and their field values are
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=(0,1),\ f=1;\qquad (x,y)=(0,-1),\ f=-1.}
$$
No division by a potentially vanishing factor of $y$ was used, so no stationary points have been discarded.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <level set> $f=c$ can be rearranged, without changing its domain, as
$$
(c+2)x^2+cy^2-2y+c=0.
$$
The requested special contours are therefore
$$
\boxed{f=0:\ y=x^2;\qquad
f=1:\ (x,y)=(0,1);\qquad
f=-1:\ y+1=\pm x.}
$$
The first is a <parabola>, the second a single point, and the last two intersecting straight lines. Moreover
$$
1-f=\frac{3x^2+(y-1)^2}{D}\geq0,
$$
with equality only at $(0,1)$. Hence $(0,1)$ is a strict global maximum. At $(0,-1)$ the crossing critical contour separates sectors with values above and below $-1$, so it is a <saddle point of a scalar function>.

As a <Hessian matrix> check, $H_f(0,1)=\operatorname{diag}(-3,-1)$ is negative definite, while $H_f(0,-1)=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$ is indefinite. There is no stationary minimum.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $c\ne0$, <completing the square> gives the <conic section>
$$
(c+2)x^2+c\left(y-\frac1c\right)^2=\frac{1-c^2}{c}.
$$
The possible levels follow from
$$
f+2=\frac{2(y^2+y+1)}{D}>0,\qquad f\leq1:
\qquad \boxed{-2<f\leq1.}
$$
For $0<c<1$, the contours are <ellipses> centred at $(0,1/c)$, shrinking to the maximum as $c\uparrow1$. The zero contour is the <parabola> from part (c). For $-1<c<0$, the contours are vertical-opening <hyperbolas>. At $c=-1$ they degenerate into the intersecting straight lines, and for $-2<c<-1$ they are horizontal-opening <hyperbolas>. These descriptions specify the remaining contour families and their symmetry about the $y$ axis. The lower bound $-2$ is approached as $|x|\to\infty$ at fixed $y$ but is never attained.

\Image[paper-2-q12-gradient-contours.png]
{title=Contours and an ascending <gradient> trajectory}
{description=Original contours of the rational scalar field. The critical points are marked; the green parabola is the zero contour and the crossing dashed lines have level $-1$. The red trajectory starts at $(1,1)$ and follows the positive <gradient> towards the maximum, as discussed in part (e).}

= e
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Use the <gradient flow> of $-f$, namely $\dot{\boldsymbol r}=\nabla f$; any positive change of speed leaves the same trajectory. At the starting point,
$$
\nabla f(1,1)=\left(-\frac43,\frac23\right),
$$
so the path initially points up and left, with slope $dy/dx=-1/2$. At every regular point it crosses the <level sets> orthogonally, because the <gradient> is normal to the contours. Along it,
$$
\frac{df}{ds}=\nabla f\cdot\dot{\boldsymbol r}=|\nabla f|^2>0
$$
until a stationary point is approached.

The red curve in the plot first rises above $y=1$, then bends down towards the maximum $(0,1)$. It stays on the side $x>0$ and approaches the maximum without crossing it in finite gradient-flow time. Indeed $\dot x=-4x(1+y+y^2)/D^2$ preserves the sign of $x$, and the critical level increases from its starting value $0$ towards $1$. The requested curve is an ascending trajectory, not a contour or a straight segment joining the initial and final points.

