Split the <vector field> into $a(-y,x,0)$ and $e^{-r^2}(x,y,z)$. The second part is the <gradient> of $-\tfrac12e^{-r^2}$, so its <curl> vanishes. The first part has only a vertical <curl> component, $\partial_x(ax)-\partial_y(-ay)=2a$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\boldsymbol F=(0,0,2a).}
$$

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <curl> found in part (a) is constant, so its <divergence> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot(\nabla\times\boldsymbol F)=0.}
$$
This also follows from the identity <divergence of a curl is zero>, applicable here because the field is smooth everywhere.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

By part (a), the <vector field> is <irrotational> exactly when $2a=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{a=0.}
$$

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The original PDF has angle $\theta(t)=2\pi t^n$, with $n$ an exponent on $t$. It is not $2\pi nt$: each permitted value of $n$ changes the speed but traverses the same circle once counterclockwise. Along the path, $r^2=2$ and
$$
\boldsymbol r'(t)=\theta'(t)(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,0),\qquad
\theta'=2\pi n t^{n-1}.
$$
The radial contribution to the <line integral> vanishes, since $(\cos\theta,\sin\theta,1)\cdot(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,0)=0$. The rotational contribution gives
$$
\boldsymbol F(\boldsymbol r(t))\cdot\boldsymbol r'(t)=a\theta'(t).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_\Gamma\boldsymbol F\cdot d\boldsymbol r
=a[\theta(1)-\theta(0)]=2\pi a,\qquad n=1,2,\ldots.}
$$
The answer is independent of the monotone parametrization, as an oriented <line integral> should be.

= e
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The upward unit <vector> extracts the vertical <curl> component:
$$
(\nabla\times\boldsymbol F)\cdot\boldsymbol k=2a.
$$
It is constant over the unit disc, so
$$
\boxed{I=\int_D2a\,dS=2\pi a.}
$$
This agrees with part (d) by <Stokes theorem>: the boundary circle has counterclockwise orientation when viewed from the upward normal, including for the parametrizations $2\pi t^n$.

= f
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

For $a=0$, differentiate the proposed <scalar potential> directly:
$$
\nabla\left(-\frac12e^{-(x^2+y^2+z^2)}\right)
=e^{-r^2}(x,y,z).
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\Phi(\boldsymbol r)=-\frac12e^{-r^2}+C\quad\text{when }a=0.}
$$
For $a\ne0$, the <curl> is the nonzero <vector> $(0,0,2a)$. A <gradient> of a twice differentiable scalar field has zero <curl>, so no such scalar field exists. Equivalently the closed <line integral> in part (d) is nonzero, contradicting the path independence of a <conservative vector field>. The field is smooth on all of $\mathbb R^3$, so no excluded singular set complicates this conclusion.

