= Solution
{parent=i}

The <probability density> is symmetric about $300\,\mathrm{mL}$, vanishes at $270$ and $330\,\mathrm{mL}$, and has its single maximum $\pi/(120\,\mathrm{mL})$ at $300\,\mathrm{mL}$. It is zero outside that interval. Its area is one: with the dimensionless <change of variables>
$$
X=\frac{\pi(v-300\,\mathrm{mL})}{60\,\mathrm{mL}},
\qquad p_X(x)=\frac12\cos x,\quad -\frac\pi2\leq x\leq\frac\pi2,
$$
normalization is $\tfrac12\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos x\,dx=1$.

\Image[paper-2-q14-coffee.png]
{title=Coffee-volume density and the two-cup tail region}
{description=Original graphs. Left: the density with the passing interval shaded. Right: transformed independent cup volumes $X,Y$ and the triangular region $X+Y\geq\pi/2$ used for the <probability> in part (b).}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Passing corresponds to $-\pi/6\leq X\leq\pi/6$ in the normalized variable from part (i). Integrating its <probability density>,
$$
\boxed{p_{\mathrm{pass}}=\frac12\int_{-\pi/6}^{\pi/6}\cos x\,dx
=\sin\frac\pi6=\frac12.}
$$
The complementary <probability> of detecting the fault in one test is also $1/2$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The absolutely convergent <geometric series> has sum $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}r^k=(1-r)^{-1}$ for $0<r<1$. Its <Cauchy product> with itself is
$$
\left(\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}r^k\right)^2
=\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\left(\sum_{k=0}^{m}1\right)r^m
=\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}(m+1)r^m.
$$
There are exactly $m+1$ pairs of nonnegative integers whose sum is $m$. Relabeling $j=m+1$ proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}j r^{j-1}=\frac1{(1-r)^2}.}
$$
Absolute convergence justifies the multiplication and regrouping; no differentiation of an infinite series is needed.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Let $T$ count tests up to and including the first failure. By <independence of random variables>, the event $T=j$ requires $j-1$ passes and then a failure. With pass <probability> $p=1/2$ and failure <probability> $q=1/2$, this is the <geometric distribution>
$$
\mathbb P(T=j)=q p^{j-1},\qquad j=1,2,\ldots.
$$
Using part (iii), its <expected value> is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[T]=q\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}jp^{j-1}
=\frac{q}{(1-p)^2}=\frac1q=2.}
$$
This counts the test that discovers the fault. The expected number of earlier passing tests alone would be one.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply the same dimensionless transformation independently to the two cups, giving $X,Y\in[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$. The total-volume condition becomes $X+Y\geq\pi/2$. Since neither variable can exceed $\pi/2$, this region has $0\leq X\leq\pi/2$ and $\pi/2-X\leq Y\leq\pi/2$, the triangle shown above.

By <independence of random variables>, the <joint probability density> is $\tfrac14\cos x\cos y$. The desired <double integral> is
$$
\begin{aligned}
P&=\frac14\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos x\int_{\pi/2-x}^{\pi/2}\cos y\,dy\,dx\\
&=\frac14\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos x(1-\cos x)\,dx\\
&=\frac14\left[1-\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{1+\cos2x}{2}\,dx\right]
=\frac14-\frac\pi{16}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(v_1+v_2\geq630\,\mathrm{mL})=\frac{4-\pi}{16}.}
$$
Boundary equality has <probability> zero for these continuous random variables.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Because the equation depends on the ratio $y/x$, set $u=y/x$, so $y'=u+xu'$. Dividing by $x^2$ and substituting gives
$$
u+xu'+u=1+u^2,\qquad x\frac{du}{dx}=(u-1)^2.
$$
This reduces the <homogeneous first-order differential equation> to a <separable differential equation>. For $u\ne1$,
$$
\int\frac{du}{(u-1)^2}=\int\frac{dx}{x},\qquad
-\frac1{u-1}=\ln x+C.
$$
At $x=e$, the condition $y=0$ gives $u=0$, so $1=1+C$ and $C=0$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=x\left(1-\frac1{\ln x}\right),\qquad 1<x<\infty.}
$$
The interval displayed is the maximal solution interval containing $x=e$: there is a pole at $x=1$. Separating variables omitted the solution $u=1$, or $y=x$, but that solution does not satisfy the given condition.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Differentiate $v'=u$ once, then substitute the other equation. The required <second-order linear differential equation> is
$$
\boxed{v''=u'=4v+2,\qquad v''-4v=2.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <characteristic equation> of the homogeneous equation is $\lambda^2-4=0$, giving exponential solutions $e^{2t}$ and $e^{-2t}$. A constant particular solution is $v_p=-1/2$. Thus the <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{v(t)=Ae^{2t}+Be^{-2t}-\frac12,\qquad A,B\in\mathbb R.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The equation $u=v'$ fixes $u$ from the same two constants:
$$
\boxed{u(t)=2Ae^{2t}-2Be^{-2t}.}
$$
Indeed $u'=4Ae^{2t}+4Be^{-2t}=4v+2$, so both original equations hold. No further independent integration constant is available in this two-dimensional <linear system of ordinary differential equations>.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The dependent variable itself does not occur, so reduce order by putting $p=y'$. Then $p'+2p=3x$, a <first-order linear differential equation>. Its <integrating factor> is $e^{2x}$:
$$
(e^{2x}p)'=3xe^{2x},\qquad
p=\frac32x-\frac34+Ce^{-2x}.
$$
Integrating once more and renaming the constants gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac34x^2-\frac34x+A+Be^{-2x}.}
$$
The two homogeneous terms are the constant solution and the exponential associated with the roots $0,-2$ of the <characteristic equation>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the <general solution> in part (i),
$$
y(0)=A+B=1,\qquad y'(0)=-\frac34-2B=0.
$$
Thus $B=-3/8$ and $A=11/8$, giving the solution of the <initial value problem>
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac34x^2-\frac34x+\frac{11}{8}-\frac38e^{-2x}.}
$$
Direct substitution checks both the differential equation and the two initial conditions.

= 16
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
