= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The logarithm is defined away from the $z$ axis, where $x^2+y^2>0$. For the <vector> potential $\psi\widehat{\boldsymbol e}_z=(0,0,\psi)$, its <curl> is $(\psi_y,-\psi_x,0)$. Since
$$
\psi_x=\frac{ax}{x^2+y^2},\qquad \psi_y=\frac{ay}{x^2+y^2},
$$
we obtain
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol u=\left(\frac{ay}{x^2+y^2},-\frac{ax}{x^2+y^2},0\right),\qquad x^2+y^2>0.}
$$
For $a>0$ this is clockwise circulation about the axis, the velocity structure of a <point vortex> in each transverse plane.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the same domain, differentiate the Cartesian components:
$$
\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol u
=-\frac{2axy}{(x^2+y^2)^2}
+\frac{2axy}{(x^2+y^2)^2}+0.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol u=0\qquad(x^2+y^2>0).}
$$
This is also an instance of <divergence of a curl is zero>, since the potential is smooth off the axis. The classical field is undefined on the axis, so that identity must not be applied there without specifying an extension.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In <cylindrical coordinates>, $x^2+y^2=r^2$ and $r>0$, so
$$
\boxed{\psi=a\ln r.}
$$
The potential <vector> has components $(A_r,A_\theta,A_z)=(0,0,a\ln r)$. Expanding the prescribed cylindrical <curl> formula gives
$$
u_r=\frac1r\partial_\theta A_z-\partial_zA_\theta=0,
\qquad u_\theta=\partial_zA_r-\partial_rA_z=-\frac ar,
\qquad u_z=\frac1r[\partial_r(rA_\theta)-\partial_\theta A_r]=0.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{(u_r,u_\theta,u_z)=(0,-a/r,0),\qquad r>0.}
$$
Since $\widehat{\boldsymbol e}_\theta=(-\sin\theta,\cos\theta,0)$, this agrees with the Cartesian result and its clockwise orientation.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <divergence> is
$$
\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol F=2(y+3)+2z=2y+2z+6.
$$
For the tetrahedral domain, convenient integration bounds are
$$
0\leq x\leq1,\qquad 0\leq y\leq1-x,\qquad
0\leq z\leq2(1-x-y).
$$
Consequently its <volume integral> is
$$
I=\int_0^1\int_0^{1-x}\int_0^{2(1-x-y)}(2y+2z+6)\,dz\,dy\,dx.
$$
Putting $h=2(1-x-y)$, the inner <integral> is $(2y+6)h+h^2=(16-4x)(1-x-y)$. Therefore
$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_0^1(16-4x)\frac{(1-x)^2}{2}\,dx\\
&=\int_0^1(8-18x+12x^2-2x^3)\,dx
=8-9+4-\frac12.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{I=\frac52.}
$$
There is an independent <centroid> check. The vertices are $(0,0,0),(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,2)$, giving volume $1/3$ and centroid $(1/4,1/4,1/2)$. An <affine function> averages to its value at the centroid, so $I=(1/3)(6+2/4+2/2)=5/2$. This is the <integral of an affine function over a simplex>.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The transpose reverses the product order, so the definition is
$$
C=A(AB)^T=AB^TA^T.
$$
If $A$ and $B$ are <orthogonal matrices>, then $A^TA=AA^T=I$ and $B^TB=BB^T=I$. Hence
$$
C^TC=(ABA^T)(AB^TA^T)
=AB(A^TA)B^TA^T=ABB^TA^T=I.
$$
Thus **$C$ is orthogonal**. The original PDF includes the transpose of the entire product $AB$, which is essential to the expression used here.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use <Einstein notation>, with repeated suffixes summed. The <matrix multiplication> and transpose give
$$
C_{ij}=A_{ik}(AB)_{jk}=A_{ik}A_{j\ell}B_{\ell k}.
$$
Interchange the free indices, then relabel the dummy indices $k,\ell$:
$$
C_{ji}=A_{jk}A_{i\ell}B_{\ell k}
=A_{j\ell}A_{ik}B_{k\ell}
=-A_{ik}A_{j\ell}B_{\ell k}=-C_{ij}.
$$
The negative sign uses $B_{k\ell}=-B_{\ell k}$. Therefore **$C$ is skew-symmetric**. No orthogonality of $A$ is needed for this part: <matrix congruence> preserves skew-symmetry.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Collecting the coefficients in the given equation order yields the <system of linear equations> in <matrix> form
$$
\boxed{L\boldsymbol r=\boldsymbol b,\qquad
L=\begin{pmatrix}1&\lambda&0\\\lambda&0&-1\\0&4&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
\boldsymbol r=\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix},\quad
\boldsymbol b=\begin{pmatrix}1\\2\\1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <determinant> is $\det L=4-\lambda^2$, so there is exactly one solution whenever $\lambda\ne\pm2$. A zero <determinant> does not alone distinguish inconsistency from infinitely many solutions, so check those values separately.

Eliminate $x$ and $z$ using $x=1-\lambda y$ and $z=1-4y$. The remaining equation is
$$
(4-\lambda^2)y=3-\lambda.
$$
For $\lambda=2$ this reads $0=1$, and for $\lambda=-2$ it reads $0=5$. Both are inconsistent. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\text{Solutions exist exactly for }\lambda\in\mathbb R\setminus\{-2,2\},}
$$
and each permitted value has a unique solution.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $\lambda\ne\pm2$, the reduced equation gives $y=(3-\lambda)/(4-\lambda^2)$. Back substitution into $x=1-\lambda y$ and $z=1-4y$ gives all solutions:
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol r=
\frac1{4-\lambda^2}\begin{pmatrix}
4-3\lambda\\3-\lambda\\-\lambda^2+4\lambda-8
\end{pmatrix},\qquad \lambda\ne\pm2.}
$$
The nonzero <determinant> proves that there are no other solutions at these parameter values.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $Y=\begin{pmatrix}p&q\\r&s\end{pmatrix}$. The <commutativity> condition gives
$$
XY=\begin{pmatrix}2r&2s\\-p&-q\end{pmatrix},\qquad
YX=\begin{pmatrix}-q&2p\\-s&2r\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Equality is equivalent to $s=p$ and $q=-2r$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{Y=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&2\beta\\-\beta&\alpha\end{pmatrix}
=\alpha I+\beta X,\qquad \alpha,\beta\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Each displayed <matrix> commutes with $X$, since it is a polynomial in $X$. The <centralizer of a nonscalar two by two matrix> explains why this two-parameter form is typical.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <matrix trace> and <determinant> of the <matrices> in part (i) are
$$
\operatorname{Tr}Y=2\alpha,\qquad \det Y=\alpha^2+2\beta^2.
$$
Since $\operatorname{Tr}X=0$ and $\det X=2$, the extra conditions force $\alpha=0$ and $\beta^2=1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{Y=X\quad\text{or}\quad Y=-X.}
$$
Both choices are real, commute with $X$, and satisfy the required trace and <determinant> conditions.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume $f\in L^2[-L,L]$, so the squared-error <integral> is finite. Write
$$
r_N(x)=f(x)-\frac{a_0}{2}-\sum_{n=1}^Na_n\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}.
$$
At a minimum, differentiating with respect to each coefficient gives
$$
0=\frac{\partial F}{\partial a_0}=-\int_{-L}^Lr_N(x)dx,
\qquad
0=\frac{\partial F}{\partial a_m}=-2\int_{-L}^Lr_N(x)\cos\frac{m\pi x}{L}dx.
$$
By <Fourier orthogonality>, the constant has squared norm $2L$, each nonconstant cosine has squared norm $L$, and all distinct modes are orthogonal. Thus
$$
\boxed{a_0=\frac1L\int_{-L}^Lf(x)dx,\qquad
 a_n=\frac1L\int_{-L}^Lf(x)\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}dx\quad(1\leq n\leq N).}
$$
These are exactly the <Fourier coefficients> of the <Fourier cosine series> of the <even function>. To check that the stationary choice is a unique global minimum, perturb the coefficients by $\delta a_n$. The residual is orthogonal to every fitted mode, so the cross terms vanish and
$$
F(\boldsymbol a+\delta\boldsymbol a)-F(\boldsymbol a)
=\frac L2(\delta a_0)^2+L\sum_{n=1}^N(\delta a_n)^2\geq0,
$$
with equality only when every perturbation is zero. The truncated series is the <orthogonal projection onto a finite-dimensional subspace> spanned by the constant and the first $N$ cosine modes.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Evenness makes every sine coefficient zero. For the constant coefficient,
$$
a_0=\frac2L\int_0^Lx\,dx=L.
$$
An <integration by parts> gives, for $n\geq1$,
$$
a_n=\frac2L\int_0^Lx\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}dx
=\frac{2L}{n^2\pi^2}[(-1)^n-1].
$$
The even coefficients vanish and the odd coefficients are $-4L/(n^2\pi^2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac L2-\frac{4L}{\pi^2}
\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{\cos((2k+1)\pi x/L)}{(2k+1)^2}.}
$$
The <periodic function> is continuous and piecewise smooth, so the <Dirichlet-Jordan convergence theorem> identifies the <Fourier series> with $f$ at every point, including the endpoints. The absolute summability of the coefficients also gives uniform convergence.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Set $x=0$ in the convergent <Fourier series> from part (b). Since $f(0)=0$ and every cosine equals one,
$$
0=\frac L2-\frac{4L}{\pi^2}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac1{(2k+1)^2}.
$$
Rearranging gives
$$
\boxed{\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac1{(2k+1)^2}=\frac{\pi^2}{8}.}
$$
Convergence at the evaluation point is essential when extracting a numerical series identity from a <Fourier series>. Here the function is continuous there and the coefficients are absolutely summable.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For a real $2L$-periodic square-integrable function with the convention $f\sim a_0/2+\sum_{n\geq1}(a_n\cos(n\pi x/L)+b_n\sin(n\pi x/L))$, <Parseval's identity> is
$$
\boxed{\frac1L\int_{-L}^L|f(x)|^2dx
=\frac{a_0^2}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(a_n^2+b_n^2).}
$$
It states that the squared <L2 norm> equals the sum of the squared components in the orthogonal <Fourier basis>, with the normalization factors shown. In the present even-function case every $b_n$ vanishes.

= e
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Here $f(x)^2=x^2$, so
$$
\frac1L\int_{-L}^Lf(x)^2dx=\frac{2L^2}{3}.
$$
Substituting the coefficients from part (b) into <Parseval's identity> gives
$$
\frac{2L^2}{3}=\frac{L^2}{2}
+\frac{16L^2}{\pi^4}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac1{(2k+1)^4}.
$$
Hence the sum over *all* positive odd integers is $\pi^4/96$. The requested series starts at $n=1$, whose first denominator is $3^4$, so it excludes the contribution $1^{-4}$:
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac1{(2n+1)^4}=\frac{\pi^4}{96}-1.}
$$

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
