= Solution
{parent=i}

The second <partial derivatives> are $f_{xx}=4$, $f_{xy}=f_{yx}=3+2y$ and $f_{yy}=2+2x+30y$. Evaluating at the origin gives the <Hessian matrix>
$$
\boxed{H_f(0,0)=\begin{pmatrix}4&3\\3&2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <determinant> of the <Hessian matrix> is $4\cdot2-3^2=-1<0$. Hence it has one positive and one negative <eigenvalue> (explicitly $3\pm\sqrt{10}$), so its quadratic form is indefinite. The cubic terms are of higher order and cannot remove these opposite local directions of change. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(0,0)\text{ is a saddle point}.}
$$
For example the quadratic term is positive along $y=0$ but negative along $y=-3x/2$, confirming the <saddle point of a scalar function> classification.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a right prism with equilateral triangular ends, the triangle area is $\sqrt3\alpha^2/4$. Thus its volume and external area are
$$
V=\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\alpha^2\beta,\qquad
A=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\alpha^2+3\alpha\beta.
$$
Apply the <Lagrange multiplier> method to $V-\lambda(A-A_0)$ with positive lengths. The stationarity equations are
$$
\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\alpha\beta=\lambda(\sqrt3\alpha+3\beta),
\qquad
\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\alpha^2=3\lambda\alpha.
$$
The second gives $\lambda=\sqrt3\alpha/12$. Substitution in the first then gives $6\beta=\sqrt3\alpha+3\beta$, or $\beta=\alpha/\sqrt3$. The area constraint becomes
$$
\frac{3\sqrt3}{2}\alpha^2=A_0=\frac92\,\mathrm{m^2}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\alpha=3^{1/4}\,\mathrm m,\qquad
\beta=3^{-1/4}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
The resulting volume is $V_{\max}=3^{3/4}/4\,\mathrm{m^3}$. As a check on the maximum, eliminating $\beta$ gives $V(\alpha)=\sqrt3A_0\alpha/12-\alpha^3/8$, whose second <derivative> is negative for every admissible positive $\alpha$; volume also tends to zero at both degenerate boundary shapes.

= 20
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the <heat equation> $\Theta_t=\kappa\Theta_{xx}$, <separation of variables> with homogeneous <Neumann boundary condition> at both ends gives spatial modes $\cos(n\pi x/L)$ and decay factors $e^{-\kappa(n\pi/L)^2t}$. The $n=0$ mode is constant and does not decay.

The initial profile already contains only two such modes:
$$
T_0\sin^2\frac{\pi x}{L}=\frac{T_0}{2}-\frac{T_0}{2}\cos\frac{2\pi x}{L}.
$$
Evolving each mode therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\Theta(x,t)=\frac{T_0}{2}\left[1-e^{-4\kappa\pi^2t/L^2}\cos\frac{2\pi x}{L}\right].}
$$
The endpoint <derivatives> vanish, and substitution checks both the equation and the initial profile. The spatial <integral> remains $LT_0/2$: no heat escapes through insulated ends, so the limiting uniform temperature is $T_0/2$.

\Image[paper-2-q20-heat.png]
{title=Insulated and zero-temperature endpoints evolve the same initial heat profile differently}
{description=Original mode calculations. Both panels start from the same squared sine. Insulated endpoints conserve the mean temperature and approach $T_0/2$; zero-temperature endpoints remove heat and the profile decays to zero. Times are expressed as $\tau=\kappa\pi^2t/L^2$.}

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

With homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary condition>, the eigenfunctions are $\sin(n\pi x/L)$ and all modes decay. Write
$$
\Theta(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}b_n\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}
\,e^{-\kappa(n\pi/L)^2t},\qquad
b_n=\frac{2T_0}{L}\int_0^L\sin^2\frac{\pi x}{L}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\,dx.
$$
Reflection about the midpoint makes every even-$n$ coefficient zero. For odd $n$, putting $u=\pi x/L$ and using the product-to-sum identity gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
b_n&=\frac{2T_0}{\pi}\int_0^\pi\sin^2u\sin(nu)\,du\\
&=\frac{2T_0}{\pi}\left[\frac1n-\frac12\left(\frac1{n+2}+\frac1{n-2}\right)\right]
=\frac{8T_0}{\pi n(4-n^2)}.
\end{aligned}
$$
In particular $b_1=8T_0/(3\pi)$ and $b_3=-8T_0/(15\pi)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\Theta(x,t)=\frac{8T_0}{\pi}
\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}
\frac{\sin((2m+1)\pi x/L)}{(2m+1)[4-(2m+1)^2]}
\,e^{-\kappa(2m+1)^2\pi^2t/L^2}.}
$$
This uses the <Fourier sine series of a squared sine>. The coefficients are $O(n^{-3})$, so the initial series converges uniformly; for $t>0$ the exponential factors also justify termwise differentiation to check the <heat equation>. Every mode vanishes at both endpoints, and the initial series gives the specified profile. Unlike part (a), there is no constant mode: the temperature tends to zero, with leading long-time term
$$
\Theta(x,t)\sim\frac{8T_0}{3\pi}\sin\frac{\pi x}{L}\,e^{-\kappa\pi^2t/L^2}.
$$
